<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1471">1471</a>–<a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as George the Bearded · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, called the Bearded, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> from 1500 to 1539 and a firm opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. He inherited his father Albert's rights as governor of Friesland, but faced constant revolt and war with Guelders there, and in 1515 sold Friesland to the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-of-saxony.jpg" alt="George of Saxony" width="330" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.Ä._-_Bildnis_Georgs_des_Bärtigen,_Herzog_von_Sachsen_(Museum_der_bildenden_Künste).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> on 27 August 1471, the eldest son of Albert the Brave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a> of the Wettin family, and of Sidonie, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. His cousin Elector Frederick the Wise of the Ernestine branch became known for protecting Luther. George received a training in theology and other branches of learning and was better educated than most princes of his day. As early as 1488 he was regent of the ducal possessions, which included the Margraviate of Meissen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, while his father fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> for the emperor. On 21 November 1496 he married Barbara Jagiellon, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Albert settled the succession at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> on 14 February 1499, hoping to prevent further partition of his lands. After his death on 12 September 1500, George succeeded him in his German territories as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a>. In 1505 an agreement with his brother Heinrich transferred the governorship of Friesland to George, but the province went on producing revolts, and in 1515 he sold it to the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> for 100,000 florins. At home he divided the duchy into districts, acted against robber-knights and reorganised the law courts. He sometimes infringed the rights of cities, and he encouraged reforms and Humanism at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1517 George gave most of his energy to church affairs. At first he was not hostile to Luther, but he turned against him, and an acrimonious correspondence followed. In 1519 he originated the Disputation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521 he added twelve complaints of his own, mainly about indulgences and annates, to the princes' list of grievances. He helped to put down the revolt of the peasants in 1525, had Lutheran books confiscated, and in 1525 formed the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> with other rulers to protect Catholic interests. Both his son-in-law Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and his brother Heinrich joined the Reformers.</p><p>His surviving sons died without heirs, Frederick shortly after his marriage in early 1539. Under the settlement of 1499 his Protestant brother Heinrich was next in line, and George sought instead to leave the duchy to Ferdinand, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 17 April 1539 before this could be carried out. He is buried with Barbara in the Georgskapelle in Meissen Cathedral, which holds an altarpiece by Lucas Cranach the Elder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George, Duke of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the second day of the battle, troops of Landgrave Philip I of Hesse and Duke <b>George of Saxony</b> defeated the peasants under their spiritual leader Thomas Müntzer.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-15">15 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>George of Saxony</b> sold his rights to the future Charles V in 1515, and from 1524 Habsburg stadtholders ruled from Leeuwarden.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-frisia">Lordship of Frisia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.16,13.48&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Meissen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4856 391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5306 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5306 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.16,13.48&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
